On Sat, Jul 24, 2021 at 5:23 PM Jason Thorpe <thorpej@me.com> wrote:

>
> > On Jul 24, 2021, at 7:51 AM, Jason Thorpe <thorpej@me.com> wrote:
> >
> > Anyway, I=E2=80=99m almost convinced it=E2=80=99s something to do with =
how we=E2=80=99re
> configuring the SCSI controller.  I=E2=80=99m doing a little more investi=
gation
> today and hopefully will have something to try out in a little while.
>
> Ok, let me explain what I mean here.
>
> There are two types interrupt triggers on EISA=E2=80=A6 edge trigger and =
level
> trigger.
>
> With edge trigger, the PIC detects the interrupt on the RISING EDGE (i.e.
> transition from low-to-high) of the interrupt line.
>
> With level trigger, the PIC detects the interrupt so long as the interrup=
t
> line is driven low (i.e. the line has a pull-up resistor enabled in this
> mode, and the interrupt lines on the devices are open-drain).
>
> Our =E2=80=9Cahb=E2=80=9D driver is always registering the interrupt hand=
ler as level
> triggered.
>
> The 1740 controller has a configuration register for its interrupts,
> INTDEF, where we can read the IRQ that it=E2=80=99s configured to use (we=
 could
> also read this from the ECU data on Alpha, and I would like to enhance th=
e
> driver to do that, but that can wait for another day).  One of the bits i=
n
> that register is called INTHIGH.  There is a comment in the ahbreg.h file=
:
>
> #define INTHIGH 0x08            /* int high=3DACTIVE (else edge) */
>
> I believe this comment is **incorrect**.  Well, *partially* incorrect.  I
> do believe it means =E2=80=9CIRQ signal is active-high=E2=80=9D, but as I=
 have explained
> above, active-high (well, transition from low-to-high) is how edge
> triggered interrupts work, so =E2=80=9Celse edge=E2=80=9D doesn=E2=80=99t=
 make sense=E2=80=A6 what that
> really should say is =E2=80=9Celse level=E2=80=9D.  I double-checked what=
 Linux does with
> this bit, and while they don=E2=80=99t define a name for it, their driver=
 treats
> this bit as "edge trigger".
>
> So, what I think is that the firmware has configured the SCSI controller
> for an active-high (i.e. =E2=80=9Cedge triggered=E2=80=9D) interrupt, whi=
ch tells the
> microcontroller on the card to hold the interrupt line low.  But we=E2=80=
=99re
> registering the handler as =E2=80=9Clevel triggered=E2=80=9D, and thus ca=
using the PIC to
> be programmed to signal an interrupt so long as that line is low.
>
> Remember how there was previously evidence of an interrupt storm?  :-)
>
> -- thorpej
>
>

