><div class=3D"gmail_quote"><div dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"gmail_attr">On Sat, J=
ul 24, 2021 at 5:23 PM Jason Thorpe &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:thorpej@me.com">t=
horpej@me.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" sty=
le=3D"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);paddi=
ng-left:1ex"><br>
&gt; On Jul 24, 2021, at 7:51 AM, Jason Thorpe &lt;<a href=3D"mailto:thorpe=
j@me.com" target=3D"_blank">thorpej@me.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>
&gt; <br>
&gt; Anyway, I=E2=80=99m almost convinced it=E2=80=99s something to do with=
 how we=E2=80=99re configuring the SCSI controller.=C2=A0 I=E2=80=99m doing=
 a little more investigation today and hopefully will have something to try=
 out in a little while.<br>
<br>
Ok, let me explain what I mean here.<br>
<br>
There are two types interrupt triggers on EISA=E2=80=A6 edge trigger and le=
vel trigger.<br>
<br>
With edge trigger, the PIC detects the interrupt on the RISING EDGE (i.e. t=
ransition from low-to-high) of the interrupt line.<br>
<br>
With level trigger, the PIC detects the interrupt so long as the interrupt =
line is driven low (i.e. the line has a pull-up resistor enabled in this mo=
de, and the interrupt lines on the devices are open-drain).<br>
<br>
Our =E2=80=9Cahb=E2=80=9D driver is always registering the interrupt handle=
r as level triggered.<br>
<br>
The 1740 controller has a configuration register for its interrupts, INTDEF=
, where we can read the IRQ that it=E2=80=99s configured to use (we could a=
lso read this from the ECU data on Alpha, and I would like to enhance the d=
river to do that, but that can wait for another day).=C2=A0 One of the bits=
 in that register is called INTHIGH.=C2=A0 There is a comment in the ahbreg=
.h file:<br>
<br>
#define INTHIGH 0x08=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 /* int high=
=3DACTIVE (else edge) */<br>
<br>
I believe this comment is **incorrect**.=C2=A0 Well, *partially* incorrect.=
=C2=A0 I do believe it means =E2=80=9CIRQ signal is active-high=E2=80=9D, b=
ut as I have explained above, active-high (well, transition from low-to-hig=
h) is how edge triggered interrupts work, so =E2=80=9Celse edge=E2=80=9D do=
esn=E2=80=99t make sense=E2=80=A6 what that really should say is =E2=80=9Ce=
lse level=E2=80=9D.=C2=A0 I double-checked what Linux does with this bit, a=
nd while they don=E2=80=99t define a name for it, their driver treats this =
bit as &quot;edge trigger&quot;.<br>
<br>
So, what I think is that the firmware has configured the SCSI controller fo=
r an active-high (i.e. =E2=80=9Cedge triggered=E2=80=9D) interrupt, which t=
ells the microcontroller on the card to hold the interrupt line low.=C2=A0 =
But we=E2=80=99re registering the handler as =E2=80=9Clevel triggered=E2=80=
=9D, and thus causing the PIC to be programmed to signal an interrupt so lo=
ng as that line is low.<br>
<br>
Remember how there was previously evidence of an interrupt storm?=C2=A0 :-)=
<br>
<br>
-- thorpej<br>
